>>
>> I'm curious what other people think about this, as I think it's
>> the most contentious part of the proposal.
>>
>> There are three locations (now is what it is now; proposed is what I proposed):
>>
>> * Stan language usage (now "_log"; proposed remove)
>>
>>  ld << cauchy_ld(y | mu, sigma);
>>
>> * user-defined functions to be used as densities (now "_log"; proposed keep)
>>

>> * Stan math lib suffixes (now "_log"; proposed keep)
>

> I'm confused what this "keep" adds.

Right now, if you define a function "foo" with a "_log" suffix,
as in "foo_log",  as we have done for our density functions,
then we allow

  y ~ foo(theta);

to be equal (mod dropped constants) to

  increment_log_prob(foo_log(y, theta));

If we change that, we break all the user-defined functions.  But
we're going to break ~ anyway (or at least deprecate it).

>  This is probably because I lost some other piece of context.  If user-defined densities have a <propto=true> or <propto=false>, then they need special treatment of some sort, otherwise they're just something you would otherwise pass to increment_log_prob()... no?  I guess they could increment ld as a side effect rather than returning the increment... but does it limit anything to disallow that? That's just a specialized version of increment_log_prob()...

increment_log_prob() is going away --- it'll be deprecated
or eliminated in Stan 3.

- Bob

