>>  https://github.com/stan-dev/stan/wiki/Stan-3-Density-Notation-and-Increments
>
> Nice.
>
> On Monday, January 4, 2016 at 9:28:17 AM UTC+2, Bob Carpenter wrote:
>> everyone's going to expect normal(y | mu, sigma)
>> to be on the linear scale because that's the convention.
>
> The usual convention is also to return normalised density, but Stan returns unnormalised, which may add confusion (and has caused confusion as seen sometimes in Stan users list).

Good point.  I thought norm=true is a better default, but it's
going to be a killer for model efficiency and since we almost
always want norm=false for just sampling or optimziation, it's
going to be very verbose.

>> cauchy_lpdf<norm=true>(y | mu, tau);   // normalized
>> cauchy_lpdf<norm=false>(y | mu, tau);  // unnormalized
>> cauchy_lpdf(y | mu, tau);              // unnormalized
>
> I just had an idea that maybe we could use BDA notation, ie, q for unnormalised density
> cauchy_lpdf(y | mu, tau);   // normalized
> cauchy_lqdf(y | mu, tau);   // unnormalized
>
> And if we want less letters in all functions, we could use
> _p, _lp, _q, _lq, _c, _lc, _cc, _lcc, _dc, _ldc, _r

I think this will cause no end of problems because of the
R / BUGS / JAGS convention:

  dnorm  :  pdf
  pnorm  :  cdf
  qnorm  :  inverse cdf
  rnorm  :  random draw

I really regret using lp__, which I was thinking of
as the p in pdf and pmf, not as a log (event) probability.

> and then we would have
> cauchy_lp(y | mu, tau);   // log, normalized
> cauchy_lq(y | mu, tau);   // log, unnormalized

I really like that this doesn't involve the verbose <norm=true>.

> or if we want to emphasize
> cauchy_l_p(y | mu, tau);   // log, normalized
> cauchy_l_q(y | mu, tau);   // log, unnormalized

The convention in the C/C++ library is mixed, between
things like log1p and lgamma, but I don't think we
need the underscore.  Nor do we need to follow C++.

- Bob

