> >
> >
> > If we do += for incrementing target, then it makes sense to use = for assignment. And it would be pretty easy for the parser to catch a <- and suggest changing it to =. And it is pretty easy for people to do a find-and-replace in their existing Stan programs. But it is a change that affects almost every line without providing a whole lot of benefit. Perhaps if there were more people in favor of the @ notation, we could keep <- for assignment.
>
> Would we also have an @increment_log_prob(...) or similar, or would the "@" notation restrict incrementing  the target density to only certain functions?  I have often used the functionality of defining arbitrary densities. with increment_log_prob...
>
> I'm not sure that question has been discussed. My intuition is that if you do
>
> @ 42;
>
> it would be conceptually equivalent to
>
> target += 42;
>
> so maybe an explicit increment_log_prob() function would not be needed.

Whatever we do, normal_lpdf(y | mu, sigma) is just going to be an
expression evaluating to a real number (or maybe a vector, but I'm leaning
against it).  So I'm envisioning a statement

   target += <expression>;

or

   @ <expression>;

Either way, it increments the log density accumulator with the value of the expression,
so any valid expression would work.

I tend to agree that symbols like @ tend to make code look like a mess
and that the "target +=" form seems much less obscure for someone
who doesn't know the language.  That's why I dropped it.

- Bob

