Oh cool, I’ve been thinking a lot about ppc’s recently as well. I think this is a finicky and somewhat unclear issue. To me, why we care about posterior predictions for new data is both for model checking (via ppc’s) and model comparison (via information criteria).

Model checking is ideal because it helps us during the iterative cycle of data analysis (e.g., by box, rubin, and andrew’s BDA introduction). Model comparison, selection, and averaging seems stranger, because technically you can do these with ppc’s, but it seems like with recent research Aki and Andrew focus on these tasks based on comparing log densities of held out data. It was never clear to me why we never use ppc’s for this; however, for certain things such as choosing the # of mixture components, it seems like they're okay with using it? (I’ve been meaning to ask you all about this for a while.)

Dustin

