On Sunday, January 24, 2016 at 11:31:10 PM UTC-5, Dustin Tran wrote:
> Targeted checks and exploratory plots (graphical checks in general) are all pretty clearly written about ppc’s in Chapter 6 in my opinion. The use case of ppc’s for model expansion (or reduction) and averaging are also clear. However, I didn’t get a good grasp of how to do model comparison: whether we should do so through ppc’s, as in the psychology example (Gelman, Meng, Stern 1996) which assess the # components of a mixture model, or by evaluating information criteria.

I think one place where posterior predictive checks make a lot of sense is when model performance (in the practical sense) is somewhat divorced from goodness of fit as measured by the posterior density.

Lately I've been working with a model where I need to: 1) estimate the shape of the CDF for some data; and 2) use the CDF to scale a count (so true_count ~ observed_count/CDF(x) ), where the original measurements and model were on x.  Turns out there's a huge right tail on x, but it's for a small proportion of x's so ultimately it doesn't do much to the scaling but it's awful to model properly.   I'm not sure how my model would play out in the context of Bob's 1/2/3 rubric (it might do ok), but in real predictions against held-out data it does very nicely (moves the horizon where we can make good predictions forward another 1.5 months).

>
> To be specific, my confusion is—barring any computational concerns—when do we use ppc’s with omnibus measures versus leave-one-out cross validation. When I dug through your and Aki and Jonah and Jessica’s papers on information criteria, I learned a lot about technique and relationships of various criteria. but at a higher level I never understood when it’s applicable over ppc’s (are they somehow used in concert with omnibus measures in ppc’s)?

