Michael brings up a great point about held-out data and posteror predictive checks. I tried thinking of LOO-CV as a special case of the partial posterior predictive check. This is similar to in BDA (2ed, p181) where it states that the expected deviance under the posterior (evaluated on all the data) can be seen as the special case of a posterior predictive check. However, how does this extend to CV settings? For example, how does it make sense to evaluate prediction by evaluating the leave-out-out posterior predictive density on generated data from another posterior predictive distribution? Is the most general procedure of LOO-CV+(partial) PPC’s as follows: generate data from the posterior predictive p( yrep | y_i), evaluate log p( yrep | y_{-i} ), and do this for each data point; then compare it to the realized discrepancy, sum log p( y_i | y_{-i} )?

Dustin

