Again, see http://arxiv.org/abs/1506.02273, especially Section 3.
Ultimately the only thing we can reasonable even approximate
is Equation 3, which is amenable to Monte Carlo approximation
if we had lots of repeated measurements (first equations in 3.2).
We don’t typically have lots of repeated measurements, so we
have to approximate further.  We can use the single measurement
we have (Section 3.2.1), we could split the data and assume that
we can simply scale them up to approximate the unsplit measurement
(Section 3.2.2) or we could permute over many possible splittings
(Section 3.2.3).  All of these additional approximations induce
new biases and variances in the estimator that can be tricky if
not impossible to quantify in practice.

