Subject: a question from jeff alstott
Message-ID: <PTliQoBRFNg.3akbUFyjDAAJ@stan-dev.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-dev/c/PTliQoBRFNg/m/3akbUFyjDAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

jeff asks:

I'm a long time Python user just starting with Stan via Pystan. I've come to Stan because I want to do a large out-of-core calculation: I want to read into RAM large datasets (16-30 million data points), fit a particular kind of model to them, then do it again, updating the model with more data.

I'm using a conditional logistic regression model, which someone else has implemented here.

I'm using a computer with 64GB of RAM and 16 CPUs.

I load in 15 million data points, and Stan seems to choke. I wait around for ages and while the machine whirs, the calculation doesn't complete. Jim told to do "optimizing" first, but I haven't found any clear guide on that (there's lots of references to "optimizing" with stan, but no how to guide with Pystan).

This is where I am right now:

from pystan import StanModel
sm = StanModel(model_code=clogit_stan) #clogit_stan is from here. This compiles without errors/warnings.

### data_dict is boring and just sets up stuff for the model
data_dict = {'N':data.shape[0],
    'n_grp': data['Agent_Entry'].nunique(),
    'n_coef': len(data_cols),
    'x': data[data_cols].values,
    'y': data['Entered'].astype('int').values,
    'grp': data['Agent_Entry'].values+1
    }

### what am I doing wrong?
op = sm.optimizing(data=data_dict)
fit = sm.sampling(data=data_dict, init=op)

If I were smart I would start with smaller data sets :-D Unfortunately I just want to jump right into the deep end, but it seems I'm missing some understanding.

Thanks!

From uf783520c8173982c at groups.google.com  Thu Jan 28 03:25:01 2016
From: uf783520c8173982c at groups.google.com (Eric Novik)
Date: Thu, 28 Jan 2016 03:25:01 +0000
Subject: Re: [stan-dev] Re: revised density/mass notation proposal
Message-ID: <GxmQL_wxtSo.GUAPByWkDAAJ@stan-dev.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <GxmQL_wxtSo.oLDlYnSLDAAJ@stan-dev.groups.google.com>
References: <GxmQL_wxtSo.qK2lsvVHDAAJ@stan-dev.groups.google.com> <GxmQL_wxtSo.oLDlYnSLDAAJ@stan-dev.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-dev/c/GxmQL_wxtSo/m/GUAPByWkDAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

My $0.000002:

I was thinking a lot about this proposed change and I must admit that I share Bob's sentiment here in that it scares the poopies out of me. The first reaction after looking at the ported model code was how ugly (wordy?) this 'target' business looked like. Of course, this is the trivial part so changing from this:

...
model {
  target += normal_lpdf(alpha | 0, 10);
  target += normal_lpdf(beta | 0, 2);
  target += cauchy_lpdf(sigma | 0, 2.5);
  target += normal_lpdf(y | alpha * x + beta, sigma);
}

to, say, this:

...
model {
  lp += normal_lpdf(alpha | 0, 10);
  lp += normal_lpdf(beta | 0, 2);
  lp += cauchy_lpdf(sigma | 0, 2.5);
  lp += normal_lpdf(y | alpha * x + beta, sigma);
}

may or may not make it aesthetically better and as Bob pointed out: "On the specific point of lp vs. target, I don't think it matters much.  It's just going to be this not-quite-a-variable thing that kind of acts like a variable and kind of doesn't. Really ugly semantically and pragmatically, that is."

On the larger issue, I totally get that the proposed notation is closer to the underlying machinery of Stan, but is it worth pissing off a lot of Stan users who invested time in learning the current notation? Again, quoting Bob:

> I think the whole thing looks ugly, and said so
> at the meeting.  I'm extremely apprehensive about
> this change.  I worry it's going to confuse and
> alienate our users (not that they weren't confused
> before --- that's what the change is for --- to unconfuse
> them).  And I know it's going to piss off everyone who's put
> effort into code and doc for the existing system.
We are all users of programming languages. A little empathy for our brothers perhaps?

Eric

