Maybe the compromise here is to confuse beginners in equal degrees (y ~
normal(...) vs. @ normal(y|..)) and to improve the clarity of the
language for advanced users?

On 01/28, Ben Goodrich wrote:
> On Thursday, January 28, 2016 at 7:37:05 PM UTC-5, Allen B. Riddell wrote:
> >
> > Can't we just say, at every opportunity that, '@' *means* "target += "
> > and implies that the _lpdf may be omitted? (And it's the only situation
> > where it can be omitted.) I would also imagine that writing
> >
> > @ normal_lpdf(y| mu, sigma)
> >
> > would be a syntax error.
> >
>
> If @ means target +=, then I don't see why we would need to support target
> +=. I don't hate the @ syntax, but it only vaguely hints of addition and
> only vaguely hints that there is some target that is being aimed at. So, if
> you know those things, then it might make sense. But y ~ normal(mu, sigma)
> currently means that and people get confused by it, so I don't know if such
> people will be less confused by @normal(y| mu, sigma).
>
> Ben
>
>
> >
> >
> > On 01/28, Bob Carpenter wrote:
> > > By "explicit", I meant explicitly indicating a sum.
> > >
> > >   y ~ normal(mu, sigma);
> > >
> > >   @ normal(y | mu, sigma);
> > >
> > >   target += normal_lpdf(y | mu, sigma);
> > >
> > > From my vantage, only the last one supplies that.
> > >
> > > - Bob
> > >
> > > > On Jan 28, 2016, at 5:58 PM, Andrew Gelman <gel...@stat.columbia.edu>
> > wrote:
> > > >
> > > > I think explicit is better than implicit too.  But I think the
> > following _is_ explicit:
> > > >
> > > > model {
> > > > @ normal(alpha | 0, 10);
> > > > @ normal(beta | 0, 2);
> > > > @ cauchy(sigma | 0, 2.5);
> > > > @ normal(y | alpha * x + beta, sigma);
> > > > }
> > > >
> > > > I also think it would fine to allow “target +=“ and to allow “_lpdf”,
> > I just don’t think they should be required as they make models horrible.
> > > >
> > > >> On Jan 28, 2016, at 4:28 PM, Allen B. Riddell <a...@ariddell.org>
> > wrote:
> > > >>
> > > >> I get that. So I'd support giving a deprecation warning (not error)
> > if
> > > >> someone uses:
> > > >>
> > > >> alpha ~ normal(0, 10);
> > > >>
> > > >> I think the change to the new functions is a good one. Explicit is
> > > >> better than implicit.
> > > >>
> > > >> On 01/28, Bob Carpenter wrote:
> > > >>> Yes, and we could have
> > > >>>
> > > >>> alpha ~ normal(0, 10);
> > > >>>
> > > >>> as even sweeter sugar (more saccharine)!
> > > >>>
> > > >>> I think the point was that people wanted to make it explicit
> > > >>> that there is a function evaluation and increment going on,
> > > >>> not something that could be confused with random-number generation.
> > > >>>
> > > >>> - Bob
> > > >>>
> > > >>>> On Jan 28, 2016, at 2:07 PM, Allen B. Riddell <a...@ariddell.org>
> > wrote:
> > > >>>>
> > > >>>> My 2cents: I really like the notation:
> > > >>>>
> > > >>>> model {
> > > >>>> @ normal(alpha | 0, 10);
> > > >>>> @ normal(beta | 0, 2);
> > > >>>> @ cauchy(sigma | 0, 2.5);
> > > >>>> @ normal(y | alpha * x + beta, sigma);
> > > >>>> }
> > > >>>>
> > > >>>> Also, can't we support both and have the simpler version be a
> > clearly
> > > >>>> defined use of syntactic sugar?
> > > >>>>
> > > >>>>
> > > >>>> On 01/27, Andrew Gelman wrote:
> > > >>>>> No, it’s not B.S.
> > > >>>>>
> > > >>>>> Comparing this:
> > > >>>>>
> > > >>>>> model {
> > > >>>>> target += normal_lpdf(alpha | 0, 10);
> > > >>>>> target += normal_lpdf(beta | 0, 2);
> > > >>>>> target += cauchy_lpdf(sigma | 0, 2.5);
> > > >>>>> target += normal_lpdf(y | alpha * x + beta, sigma);
> > > >>>>> }
> > > >>>>>
> > > >>>>> to this:
> > > >>>>>
> > > >>>>> model {
> > > >>>>> @ normal(alpha | 0, 10);
> > > >>>>> @ normal(beta | 0, 2);
> > > >>>>> @ cauchy(sigma | 0, 2.5);
> > > >>>>> @ normal(y | alpha * x + beta, sigma);
> > > >>>>> }
> > > >>>>>
> > > >>>>> is a big difference.
> > > >>>>>
> > > >>>>> The first code snippet is hard to read, hard to write, and easy to
> > typo.  Not everything in the language is a pdf, but most things are, and
> > that’s one of the points of having a default.  I really think the top code
> > snippet is a disaster.
> > > >>>>>
> > > >>>>> I _do_ think we should change our notation, I _do_ think the
> > current Bugs-like notation is confusing because it implies something else,
> > and I _do_ think we can do it without cluttering our code with “target” and
> > “_lpdf”.
> > > >>>>>
> > > >>>>>
> > > >>>>>> On Jan 27, 2016, at 11:10 PM, Michael Betancourt <
> > betan...@gmail.com> wrote:
> > > >>>>>>
> > > >>>>>> Hey, if people want to limit the language so that everything is a
> > pdf or that we never need a Jacobian then go ahead.  But all of this talk
> > about making the language harder to read is BS -- all we are doing is
> > making the existing ambiguities explicit.  Expressiveness vs simplicity is
> > a fundamental trade-off made only trickier by the subtleties of probability
> > theory.
> > > >>>>>>
> > > >>>>>> On Jan 27, 2016, at 22:04, Andrew Gelman <
> > gel...@stat.columbia.edu <mailto:gel...@stat.columbia.edu>> wrote:
> > > >>>>>>
> > > >>>>>>> I’m with Eric.  To me, the _lpdf thing is close to a
> > deal-breaker.  Almost everything we are using is an lpdf.  I hadn’t thought
> > so much about it, but seeing Eric’s example, I agree that it’s a lot less
> > user friendly, it’s just a pain in the ass.
> > > >>>>>>>
> > > >>>>>>> If we were setting up models using graphical model expressions,
> > and this was intermediate code, I wouldn’t be so bothered.  But I really
> > really don’t want to be asking people to write their Stan code with “target
> > +=“ on each line and “_lpdf” for each probability assignment.  This truly
> > does feel like a step into oblivion.
> > > >>>>>>>
> > > >>>>>>> Consider, for example, the whole Stata vs Stan thing.  All of a
> > sudden, we’re looking like chumps. Sure, we’re faster and more scalable
> > than Stata, but our notation makes it look like you need a dual PhD in stat
> > and CS to write a model.
> > > >>>>>>>
> > > >>>>>>> A
> > > >>>>>>>
> > > >>>>>>>> On Jan 27, 2016, at 10:58 PM, Daniel Lee <bea...@alum.mit.edu
> > <mailto:bea...@alum.mit.edu>> wrote:
> > > >>>>>>>>
> > > >>>>>>>> With the examples, I just can't help wanting to add:
> > > >>>>>>>> return target;
> > > >>>>>>>> at the end of the block. I see the model block as a function,
> > so I naturally want to return something.
> > > >>>>>>>>
> > > >>>>>>>> Instead of @, I think << would be more natural. It's used in
> > c++ as the insertion operator. It's close to the scoping assignment in R
> > (<<-).
> > > >>>>>>>>
> > > >>>>>>>>
> > > >>>>>>>>
> > > >>>>>>>> I'm still in favor of the more explicit _lpdf / _lpmf suffix.
> > > >>>>>>>>
> > > >>>>>>>>
> > > >>>>>>>>
> > > >>>>>>>>
> > > >>>>>>>>
> > > >>>>>>>> On Wed, Jan 27, 2016 at 10:41 PM, Andrew Gelman <
> > gel...@stat.columbia.edu <mailto:gel...@stat.columbia.edu>> wrote:
> > > >>>>>>>> How about this:
> > > >>>>>>>>
> > > >>>>>>>> model {
> > > >>>>>>>> @ normal(alpha | 0, 10);
> > > >>>>>>>> @ normal(beta | 0, 2);
> > > >>>>>>>> @ cauchy(sigma | 0, 2.5);
> > > >>>>>>>> @ normal(y | alpha * x + beta, sigma);
> > > >>>>>>>> }
> > > >>>>>>>>
> > > >>>>>>>> Clean!
> > > >>>>>>>>
> > > >>>>>>>>
> > > >>>>>>>>> On Jan 27, 2016, at 10:25 PM, Eric Novik <eric....@gmail.com
> > <mailto:eric....@gmail.com>> wrote:
> > > >>>>>>>>>
> > > >>>>>>>>> My $0.000002:
> > > >>>>>>>>>
> > > >>>>>>>>> I was thinking a lot about this proposed change and I must
> > admit that I share Bob's sentiment here in that it scares the poopies out
> > of me. The first reaction after looking at the ported model code was how
> > ugly (wordy?) this 'target' business looked like. Of course, this is the
> > trivial part so changing from this:
> > > >>>>>>>>>
> > > >>>>>>>>> ...
> > > >>>>>>>>> model {
